userStories.description:
**Goal** — Compose a guarded per-run memory host with a run-stage provider, start sync and a freshly rendered optional index.

**Motivation** — Feature 2 made memory real for `nax chat`; this feature makes the same notes visible, and writable, in the sessions that do a run's work.

**Approach** — New module file `packages/nax/src/memory/run-memory.ts`, exported from the `@/memory` barrel:

```ts
/** The origin a note saved by a run session records. */
export const RUN_MEMORY_SOURCE = "nax-run";
/** The pipeline stage the run memory's tools attach to. */
export const RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES: readonly string[] = ["run"];

export interface RunMemorySummary {
  readonly saved: number;   // saves that returned a result during this run
  readonly pending: number; // of those, saves whose result status was "pending"
}

export interface RunMemory {
  readonly host: MemoryHost;
  /** The four nax-memory tools over a counting view of `host`, stages ["run"]. */
  readonly provider: ToolProvider;
  /** The rendered index, read fresh on every call; undefined when empty or unavailable. Never throws. */
  index(): Promise<string | undefined>;
  /** The counts `close()` logs; read by tests, no other production reader. */
  summary(): RunMemorySummary;
  /** First call: one sync, then one summary log line. Later calls do nothing. Never throws. */
  close(): Promise<void>;
}

export interface CreateRunMemoryInput {
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  /** Absolute repository root (the run's workdir); the mirror identity. */
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly logger: MemoryLogger;
}

export async function createRunMemory(input: CreateRunMemoryInput): Promise<RunMemory | undefined>;
export const _runMemoryDeps: { createMemoryHost: typeof createMemoryHost };
```

`createRunMemory`, in order:

1. `memoryConfigOf(input.config).inRun === false` → returns `undefined` without calling `createMemoryHost`.
2. `_runMemoryDeps.createMemoryHost({ config, repoRoot, source: RUN_MEMORY_SOURCE, logger })`. `null` (memory disabled) → `undefined`. A throw → `logger.warn("run", "memory unavailable", { storyId: "_run", error })` and `undefined`.
3. `await host.sync()` once. A throw → `logger.warn("run", "memory sync failed", { storyId: "_run", error })`; the run memory is still returned. A returned `locked`, `unreachable` or `failed` result is not a throw and logs nothing.
4. Returns the run memory.

`index()` returns `(await host.renderIndex()).text`, or `undefined` when that text is `""`; a throw → `logger.warn("run", "memory index unavailable", { storyId: "_run", error })` and `undefined`.

`errorMessage` (`@nathapp/nax-agent/internal`) renders every `error` field.

Staged implementation boundary, verbatim: Creates `src/memory/run-memory.ts` with `createRunMemory`, `RunMemory`, `RunMemorySummary`, `CreateRunMemoryInput`, `RUN_MEMORY_SOURCE`, `RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES`, `_runMemoryDeps` and `index()`; `provider`, `summary()` and `close()` are present with their final types and are completed by US-004 (until then `summary()` returns `{ saved: 0, pending: 0 }`, `close()` resolves without doing anything, and `provider` is the uncounted host's provider with `RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES`); exports from the `@/memory` barrel. The interface comments describe the final contract; counting and close semantics are intentionally deferred to US-004. Construct a new provider object (`{ ...provider, stages: RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES }`), not a mutation of the feature-2 provider.

Integration, read only: `createMemoryHost(input: CreateMemoryHostInput): Promise<MemoryHost | null>` and `MemoryHost` (`src/memory/host.ts`); `CreateMemoryHostInput { config: NaxConfig; repoRoot: string; source: string; logger: MemoryLogger }`. `createMemoryToolProvider(host: MemoryHost): ToolProvider` (`src/memory/tool-provider.ts:354`), `kind: "static"`, `stages: ["*"]`, id `MEMORY_TOOL_PROVIDER_ID` (`"nax-memory"`).

The tools attach by provider stage: the run memory's provider declares `stages: ["run"]`, and the existing stage filter (`providerAttachesTo`, `packages/nax-agent/src/tools/provider-types.ts:71`, reached through `resolveProviderContribution` with the op's `pipelineStage`) keeps it off every `review`-stage session. No session-role list is added.

Stories run one at a time in workflow mode (`effectiveParallelCount` returns 0, `src/execution/workflow-parallel.ts`), and a static provider's tools run in the nax host process, not in the sandbox (`packages/nax-agent/src/tools/provider-adapt.ts:34`). So a run-stage save writes the mirror directly, exactly like a chat save; the design's "stages only append outbox files" rule has nothing to guard here.

**Scope** — In: new run-memory.ts and test/unit/memory/run-memory.test.ts, and barrel exports of createRunMemory, _runMemoryDeps, RUN_MEMORY_SOURCE, RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES and types RunMemory, RunMemorySummary, CreateRunMemoryInput. Out: save counting/end sync/summary logging (US-004), runtime carrying/close ownership (US-005), setup (US-006), prompt builders/callers (US-002/US-007). No change to packages/nax-agent or existing permission behavior. No memory.stages configuration, role lists, mid-session sync or new memory providers.

**Interface** — The TypeScript block above is the final exported surface. Use memoryConfigOf for the switch, the existing MemoryLogger and ToolProvider types, and errorMessage for every warning.

Testing: write failing unit tests through _runMemoryDeps with restored seams, including host creation failure, disabled/null cases, guarded start sync and fresh render failures. In ACs, partial memory blocks denote overrides on a fully resolved NaxConfig fixture (use the existing makeNaxConfig helper or schema parsing); do not pass an incomplete MemoryConfig to the typed factory. Test memory.enabled: false through the real disabled-host path, since createRunMemory delegates that switch to createMemoryHost rather than checking it itself. With a real host over temporary directories assert the four provider tool names/order. The test suite created here is extended, not recreated, by US-004.

**Goal** — Complete the run memory's counting tool-provider view and idempotent end-of-run sync and summary.

**Motivation** — What an earlier chat or run worked out (a test command that needs a container, a review ruling, a reason a fix was reverted) is in the memory mirror, but no run session sees it, and a run session cannot save what the next one would need.

**Approach** — `provider` is `createMemoryToolProvider(counting)` with its `stages` replaced by `RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES` (a new object: `{ ...provider, stages: RUN_MEMORY_TOOL_STAGES }`). `counting` is `host` with `save` wrapped: after `host.save(write)` resolves, `saved` grows by one, and `pending` by one when the result's `status` is `"pending"`; a rejected save changes neither. Every other `MemoryHost` member is the host's own.

`close()` on its first call awaits `host.sync()` (a throw → the same `memory sync failed` warn), then logs `logger.info("run", "Memory: <saved> saved (<pending> pending)", { storyId: "_run", saved, pending })`. A second call returns without syncing or logging.

`errorMessage` (`@nathapp/nax-agent/internal`) renders every `error` field.

Failure Handling:
- a save is rejected by the store (secret, invalid note) → the tool answers `isError` (feature 2); the counter does not change.
- run-end `host.sync()` throws → warn `memory sync failed`; the summary line is still logged; `runtime.close()` continues.

The tools attach by provider stage: the run memory's provider declares `stages: ["run"]`, and the existing stage filter (`providerAttachesTo`, `packages/nax-agent/src/tools/provider-types.ts:71`, reached through `resolveProviderContribution` with the op's `pipelineStage`) keeps it off every `review`-stage session. No session-role list is added.

Stories run one at a time in workflow mode (`effectiveParallelCount` returns 0, `src/execution/workflow-parallel.ts`), and a static provider's tools run in the nax host process, not in the sandbox (`packages/nax-agent/src/tools/provider-adapt.ts:34`). So a run-stage save writes the mirror directly, exactly like a chat save; the design's "stages only append outbox files" rule has nothing to guard here.

**Scope** — In: complete provider, summary() and close() in the US-003 run-memory.ts; extend its existing run-memory.test.ts. Preserve the startup sync and index behavior from US-003. Out: runtime ownership and finally integration (US-005), setup (US-006), prompt delivery (US-002/US-007). No new validation/secret policy, permission handling or packages/nax-agent changes. No sync between stories or mid-session. The summary remains only a close-time log line, not a workflow/footer/status/PR change.

**Interface** — Final types, already introduced by US-003:
```ts
export interface RunMemorySummary {
  readonly saved: number;   // saves that returned a result during this run
  readonly pending: number; // of those, saves whose result status was "pending"
}

export interface RunMemory {
  readonly host: MemoryHost;
  /** The four nax-memory tools over a counting view of `host`, stages ["run"]. */
  readonly provider: ToolProvider;
  /** The rendered index, read fresh on every call; undefined when empty or unavailable. Never throws. */
  index(): Promise<string | undefined>;
  /** The counts `close()` logs; read by tests, no other production reader. */
  summary(): RunMemorySummary;
  /** First call: one sync, then one summary log line. Later calls do nothing. Never throws. */
  close(): Promise<void>;
}
```
`MemorySaveResult { slug: string; status: MemoryNoteStatus; delivered: boolean }` (`packages/nax-agent/src/memory/operations.ts:126`). Keep RunMemory.host as the original host, not the counting view.

Testing: exercise save and forget by invoking actual provider tools rather than directly calling a wrapper; assert forwarded note fields, fulfilled/rejected counters, sync totals, exact structured log calls, and first/second close behavior including a rejecting end sync. Status-only result literals in ACs denote the observed subset: fake saves must return a complete MemorySaveResult with slug, status and delivered. The model supplies slug/type/description/body and optional scope; the existing tool provider builds MemoryNoteWrite (including the default scope), and the counting view forwards that write to host.save unchanged. Do not stamp source in the wrapper: the existing host already records RUN_MEMORY_SOURCE from composition. Restore _runMemoryDeps after each case. Set the close guard before awaiting sync to keep first-call-only behavior under overlap.
